[38;5;231m⏺[39m One thing I should flag before the last question, because it undercuts the feature if we don't face it: in a
  client-only single-user app, [1myou are the only source of change[0m. A toast saying "you marked this done" one millisecond
  after you clicked "done" is noise, not a notification. The feature only earns its place when a change arrives from
  somewhere you weren't watching.

  There are three honest ways it does that today: another browser tab of the same app (cross-tab sync makes this
  demonstrably real — two tabs behave like two clients), time passing (a due date arriving is a change no one clicked),
  and later, a server. That's worth designing for now, since it's also exactly what validates the seam.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether a notification is a moment or a record. Ephemeral means no storage and no read state; persistent
  means a [38;5;153mnotifications[39m store with read/unread, which is also the thing a future server would sync.

  - [1mPersistent inbox[0m — bell with unread count, list you can review, survives reload. Costs a store and read-state
    handling. Makes changes-while-away visible, which is the only version of this that works across sessions.
  - [1mEphemeral toasts[0m — cheapest, zero storage, but anything generated while the tab was closed never existed. In a
    single-user app that's most of them.
  - [1mBoth[0m — toast on arrival for presence, inbox for history. Slightly more UI, no extra data model beyond the inbox.

  [1mMy recommendation: both[0m, with the inbox as the source of truth and the toast as a view of a just-arrived record. Plus
  cross-tab propagation via the [38;5;153mstorage[39m event and a due-date check on load, so the system has real change sources today
  instead of only after a backend exists.
